From therneau at mayo.edu  Fri Feb  4 17:16:04 2005
From: therneau at mayo.edu (Terry Therneau)
Date: Fri, 04 Feb 2005 11:16:04 -0600
Subject: recoding
Message-ID: <200502041716.j14HG4909137@prolapse.mayo.edu>

For recoding a set of variables:
 I'll assume that all of these variables reside in a particular data frame
named "mydata", and that you want to change the variable levels:

   for (i in c('a1', 'a2', 'a3', 'a4') )
        mydata[[i]] <- ifelse(mydata[[i]]==1, 1, 0)
        
 Or, perhaps you want to create new variable names:
 
   oldname <- c('a1', 'a2', 'a3', 'a4')
   newname <- c('b1', 'b2', 'b3', 'b4')
   for (i in 1:4)
        mydata[[newname[i]]] <- 2- mydata[[oldname[[i]]]        
        
        
 Another example, but with the variables not in a data frame
   for (i in 1:length(oldname)) {
        old <- get(oldname[i])
        new <- 2*(old=='Yes') + 0*(old=='No') + 1*(old='Maybe')
        assign(newname[i], new)
        }
        
Using character string names is actually more flexible than SAS arrays,
and just as easy (once someone shows you).  

        Terry Therneau
        
                
        

